.TH tkulauncher n 0.2 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkulauncher
.PP
An application launcher widget, in two shapes from one widget: a \fBmenu\fR (a ttk::menubutton with nested cascades \-\- a "Start" menu) or a \fBlist\fR (a vertical column of ttk::buttons \-\- a launcher panel). Entries start a program, open a URL, or open a file/folder. \fBCross\-platform\fR: URLs and files are opened with the OS default through \fBtclutils::tuopen\fR (xdg\-open / open / cmd start), so nothing is tied to a desktop environment \-\- the same launcher works on Windows and Linux. Tk 8.6+ and 9.x.
.SS API
.CS
::tkutils::tkulauncher::widget  path ?options?
::tkutils::tkulauncher::load    file       ;# parse a .json/.ini spec -> items
::tkutils::tkulauncher::resolve entry      ;# what an entry would launch (no side effect)

::tkutils::tkulauncher::reload       path  ;# re-read the -file spec and rebuild
::tkutils::tkulauncher::editConfig   path  ;# open the config file in an editor
::tkutils::tkulauncher::openLocation path  ;# open the folder holding the config
::tkutils::tkulauncher::showCalendar   path  ;# open the built-in calendar window
::tkutils::tkulauncher::showCalculator path  ;# open the built-in calculator window
::tkutils::tkulauncher::editEntries   path  ;# open the form-based entry editor
::tkutils::tkulauncher::saveEntries   items file  ;# write entries (JSON or INI by extension)
::tkutils::tkulauncher::systemIds            ;# all known system ids (for pickers)
.CE
.SS Options
.IP \(bu
\fB\-mode\fR     \fBmenu\fR or \fBlist\fR (default \fBlist\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-text\fR     menubutton label in menu mode (default \fBStart\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-items\fR    the entry spec (a Tcl list of dicts).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-file\fR     load the spec from a \fB.json\fR or \fB.ini\fR file instead.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-onlaunch\fR optional script, appended \fB{kind argv}\fR just before launching. A false return suppresses the actual launch \-\- useful for logging, confirmation, or testing.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-system\fR when true, append a \fBSystem\fR group of environment\-aware tools (terminal, file manager, task manager, printers, autostart, ...) that resolve to the right command for the running platform / desktop.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-columns\fR (list mode) lay the buttons out in N columns instead of one, so a small panel of important entries stays compact. Section headers and separators span all columns. Combine with \fB\-scroll\fR if needed.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-tooltips\fR show a tooltip on each list button: an entry's explicit \fBtooltip\fR field, or (when on) one derived from what the entry would launch (the command, URL, or resolved system command). Handy when labels are short.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-scroll\fR (list mode) put the buttons in a scrollable frame with a scrollbar, so a long list stays usable; \fB\-height\fR sets its pixel height (default 400). The \fBmouse wheel\fR scrolls the panel from anywhere in it (via \fBtkuwheel\fR), and keeps working after a reload.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-settings\fR when true and a \fB\-file\fR is in use, append a \fBSettings\fR group (submenu in menu mode, section in list mode) with \fIEdit menu...\fR, \fIOpen file\fR\fI \fR\fIlocation\fR, and \fIReload\fR.
.SS "Entry spec"
.PP
A list of dicts, each with a \fBtype\fR:
.CS
{type app  label "Firefox" cmd {firefox} icon path dir path tooltip text}  ;# run a program
{type url       label "Manual"   target https://tcl.tk}  ;# open a URL
{type open      label "Home"     target /home/greg}      ;# open a file/folder
{type separator}                                          ;# a divider
{type menu      label "Internet" items {...}}            ;# a submenu / section
{type system    id taskmanager}                         ;# an environment-aware system tool
{type calendar}                                         ;# open a built-in calendar window
{type calc}                                             ;# open a built-in calculator window
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBapp\fR \-\- \fBcmd\fR is a program and its arguments; run in the background.
.IP \(bu
\fBurl\fR / \fBopen\fR \-\- \fBtarget\fR is opened with the OS default application.
.IP \(bu
\fBseparator\fR \-\- a divider.
.IP \(bu
\fBmenu\fR \-\- in \fBmenu\fR mode a cascade (submenu); in \fBlist\fR mode a section header (a bold label) followed by its items.
.SS "Two shapes"
.CS
package require tkutils::tkulauncher

set spec {
    {type menu label Internet items {
        {type app label Firefox cmd firefox}
        {type url label "Tcl Manual" target https://www.tcl.tk/man/tcl9.0/}
    }}
    {type separator}
    {type app label Terminal cmd xterm}
}

# a Start menu:
::tkutils::tkulauncher::widget .start -mode menu -text Start -items $spec
pack .start

# or a launcher panel:
::tkutils::tkulauncher::widget .panel -mode list -items $spec
pack .panel -fill both -expand 1
.CE
.SS "From a file"
.CS
::tkutils::tkulauncher::widget .l -mode list -file ~/.config/mylauncher.json
.CE
.PP
\fBJSON\fR \-\- an object \fB{"items":[...]}\fR or a bare array of entries:
.CS
{ "items": [
    { "type": "menu", "label": "Internet", "items": [
        { "type": "app", "label": "Firefox", "cmd": ["firefox"] },
        { "type": "url", "label": "Manual", "target": "https://tcl.tk" }
    ]},
    { "type": "app", "label": "Terminal", "cmd": ["xterm"] }
]}
.CE
.PP
\fBINI\fR \-\- each \fB[section]\fR is a submenu; each \fBkey = value\fR is an entry, with a \fBtype:\fR prefix picking the type (default \fBapp\fR):
.CS
[Internet]
Firefox = app: firefox
Manual  = url: https://tcl.tk

[System]
Terminal = xterm
Home     = open: /home/greg
TaskMgr  = system: taskmanager
.CE
.PP
JSON is parsed through \fBtclutils::tujson\fR, INI through \fBtclutils::tuini\fR.
.SS "Settings: edit, locate, reload"
.PP
A launcher built from a file can manage its own configuration. Either turn on \fB\-settings\fR for a ready\-made Settings group, or wire these to your own buttons / menu entries:
.CS
::tkutils::tkulauncher::editConfig   .l   ;# open the config in $EDITOR / notepad
::tkutils::tkulauncher::openLocation .l   ;# open the folder it lives in
::tkutils::tkulauncher::reload       .l   ;# re-read it and rebuild in place
.CE
.PP
The usual flow is \fIEdit menu...\fR (opens the file), make changes, \fIReload\fR (the launcher rebuilds without a restart). Editing and locating go through \fBtclutils::tuopen\fR, so they use the OS editor and file manager on both Windows and Linux. Without a \fB\-file\fR, these report that there is nothing to edit.
.SS "Long menus"
.PP
In \fBlist\fR mode, a long button column can overflow the window; pass \fB\-scroll 1\fR (optionally with \fB\-height\fR) to put it in a scrollable frame with a scrollbar. In \fBmenu\fR mode, Tk scrolls an over\-long menu natively (arrows); the better fix there is to group entries into submenus with \fBtype menu\fR.
.SS "Icons, working directory, availability"
.PP
App and other entries take a few optional fields:
.IP \(bu
\fBicon\fR \-\- a path to an image file (png/gif/...) or the name of an existing Tk image; shown next to the label in both modes. A missing/unreadable icon is ignored (the entry just shows text).
.IP \(bu
\fBdir\fR \-\- a working directory the program is launched in (app entries).
.IP \(bu
\fBterminal\fR \-\- run a CLI program inside a terminal window (app entries): on Windows a new console (\fBcmd /k\fR), on Linux the desktop terminal with \fB\-e\fR (or \fB\-\-\fR for gnome\-terminal). Use for htop, less, a REPL, etc.
.PP
On \fBWindows\fR, app entries are launched through \fBstart\fR, so a program that is registered under \fIApp Paths\fR (Firefox, Chrome, ...) works even though it is not on \fBPATH\fR \-\- you do not need to give a full path. A working directory is passed via \fBstart /D\fR. On \fBLinux/macOS\fR the program is run directly (it must be on \fBPATH\fR or given as a full path).
.PP
App entries whose program is \fBnot found\fR are shown \fBgreyed out\fR (disabled), so a menu never offers something that would fail. On Windows a bare name is assumed launchable (start resolves it); only an explicit path that does not exist is greyed out. \fBavailable $entry\fR reports this (url/open/system entries are always available, since they go through the OS opener).
.CS
{type app label "Editor" cmd {code} icon ~/.icons/code.png dir ~/projects}
::tkutils::tkulauncher::available {type app label X cmd firefox}   ;# 1 or 0
.CE
.SS "Editing the menu with a form"
.PP
When a launcher is built with \fB\-file\fR and \fB\-settings 1\fR, the Settings submenu offers "Edit menu (form)..." next to the plain text editor. A launcher built with \fB\-editable 1\fR (even from \fB\-items\fR, with no Settings group) can also be edited by right\-clicking it and choosing "Edit..." \-\- handy for a list\-mode panel. The Type field is a dropdown, and for \fBsystem\fR entries the System id field is a dropdown of every known system id (sound, bluetooth, display, ...), so you pick instead of type. It opens a form\-based editor (needs \fBtkutils::tkuform\fR): a list of entries on the left, a form on the right. New/Delete manage the list, and a "New in:" dropdown chooses which menu a new entry is created in. Up/Down reorder the selected entry within its menu, and "Move to..." moves it into another menu (top level or any submenu). Apply copies the form into the selected entry; "Update launcher" applies the changes to the live launcher; and "Save to file..." writes them back \-\- as JSON (full fidelity) or INI (flat prefix form) chosen by the file extension \-\- then reloads the launcher.
.CS
::tkutils::tkulauncher::editEntries .launcher    ;# open the editor
::tkutils::tkulauncher::saveEntries $items menu.json  ;# or menu.ini
.CE
.PP
Saving is symmetric with loading: \fBmenu.json\fR round\-trips every field including nested submenus; \fBmenu.ini\fR maps top\-level menus to sections and entries to \fBlabel = prefix: value\fR lines (\fBapp:\fR, \fBurl:\fR, \fBopen:\fR, \fBfile:\fR, \fBsystem:\fR).
.SS "Calendar and calculator"
.PP
A \fBcalendar\fR entry opens a small calendar window, and a \fBcalc\fR entry opens the built\-in calculator (\fBtkucalc\fR, with keyboard input and history) \-\- both in a reusable window that stays above the launcher (a transient satellite of it) and minimizes with it.
.PP
The calendar picks the richest widget available: if the \fBtical\fR engine is on the auto_path it uses \fBtkutical\fR (month view with week numbers and German holidays), otherwise the dependency\-free clickable \fBtkucalendar\fR, then the text \fBtkucal\fR, then a built\-in month grid. So a launcher works with no external programs, and gets more when tical is present.
.CS
{type calendar}   ;# label defaults to "Calendar"
{type calc}       ;# label defaults to "Calculator"
.CE
.SS Calendar
.PP
A \fBcalendar\fR entry opens a small calendar window (month view with previous/next/today). It uses the tkutils \fBtkucal\fR widget when available and falls back to a dependency\-free month grid otherwise \-\- so it works everywhere, like the rest of the launcher.
.CS
{type calendar}                 ;# label defaults to "Calendar"
{type calendar label "Kalender"}
.CE
.SS "System tools (environment\-aware)"
.PP
A \fBsystem\fR entry names a well\-known tool by \fBid\fR, and the launcher chooses the right command for the running platform and desktop \-\- so one menu does the right thing on Windows 11, XFCE, GNOME or KDE:
.CS
{type system id terminal}      ;# xfce4-terminal / gnome-terminal / konsole / wt.exe
{type system id taskmanager}   ;# xfce4-taskmanager / gnome-system-monitor / taskmgr.exe
{type system id printers}      ;# system-config-printer / ms-settings:printers
{type system id autostart}     ;# xfce4-session-settings / shell:startup / ~/.config/autostart
.CE
.PP
Known ids: \fBterminal\fR, \fBcmd\fR (Windows), \fBpowershell\fR, \fBfilemanager\fR, \fBtaskmanager\fR, \fBsettings\fR, \fBprinters\fR, \fBautostart\fR, \fBdisplay\fR, \fBnetwork\fR, \fBsound\fR, \fBbluetooth\fR, \fBpower\fR, \fBkeyboard\fR, \fBmouse\fR, \fBappearance\fR, \fBdatetime\fR, \fBusers\fR, \fBabout\fR, \fBcalculator\fR, \fBeditor\fR, \fBscreenshot\fR, \fBlock\fR, \fBdiskmanager\fR, \fBcontrolpanel\fR (Windows), \fBdevicemanager\fR (Windows), \fBservices\fR (Windows), \fBsoftware\fR, \fBprintjobs\fR, \fBsysteminfo, logout, restart, shutdown, suspend, brightness, wifi, updates, firewall, fonts, notifications, colorpicker, magnifier, onscreenkeyboard, clipboard, environment\fR. An entry with no \fBlabel\fR gets a built\-in title. Ids that have no command on the current platform (e.g. \fBcmd\fR on Linux) are simply omitted by \fB\-system\fR / \fBsystemItems\fR.
.CS
# ready-made group of the tools available here (default set: terminal,
# filemanager, taskmanager, settings, printers, printjobs, network, sound,
# bluetooth, power, display, autostart, systeminfo -- unavailable ones omitted):
::tkutils::tkulauncher::widget .l -mode list -items $spec -system 1

# or build the spec yourself and embed it:
set sys [::tkutils::tkulauncher::systemItems -ids {terminal taskmanager printers}]

# resolve one id to a command without launching (override platform for testing):
::tkutils::tkulauncher::resolveSystem taskmanager -platform windows
# -> kind exec argv {taskmgr.exe}

# the display title for a system id (used when an entry gives no label):
::tkutils::tkulauncher::systemTitle taskmanager     ;# -> "Task Manager"
.CE
.PP
The commands are sensible defaults; adjust the \fBsysCat\fR catalogue for site specifics. \fBresolveSystem\fR takes \fB\-platform\fR / \fB\-os\fR / \fB\-desktop\fR overrides.
.SS resolve
.PP
\fBresolve\fR turns an entry into an action dict \fB{kind exec|open argv {...}}\fR without performing it, so a caller (or a test) can see what a click would do:
.CS
::tkutils::tkulauncher::resolve {type url label X target https://tcl.tk}
# -> kind open argv https://tcl.tk
.CE
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBtclutils::tuopen\fR, \fBtclutils::tujson\fR, \fBtclutils::tuini\fR, \fBtkumenu\fR
.SS "Launching Tcl/Tk scripts (\fBtcl\fR entries)"
.PP
A \fBtcl\fR entry runs a Tcl/Tk script with a chosen interpreter, cross\-platform. Fields: \fBtarget\fR (the script path), \fBinterp\fR (one of \fBwish9\fR, \fBwish8\fR, \fBtclsh9\fR, \fBtclsh8\fR; default \fBwish9\fR), optional \fBtcllibpath\fR (set as \fBTCLLIBPATH\fR for the child \-\- the essence of a tcl8env/tcl9env setup), optional \fBargs\fR, \fBdir\fR, and \fBterminal\fR (for \fBtclsh\fR scripts). Interpreter keys map per platform:
.TS
l l l.
key	Linux	Windows
wish9	wish9.0	wish90.exe
wish8	wish8.6	wish86t.exe
tclsh9	tclsh9.0	tclsh90.exe
tclsh8	tclsh8.6	tclsh86t.exe
.TE
.PP
The command \fBtclInterpreters\fR returns the available interpreter keys (used by the editor dropdown), \fBresolveTclInterp key ?platform?\fR maps a key to the executable for a platform, and \fBsetTclInterp\fR overrides a mapping.
.PP
Override a mapping (e.g. an absolute path) with \fB::tkutils::tkulauncher::setTclInterp wish9 \-linux /opt/tcl9/bin/wish9.0 \-windows C:/Tcl/bin/wish90.exe\fR. Example entry: \fB{type tcl label "My App" target ~/app.tcl interp wish9 tcllibpath "~/lib/tcl9.0 ~/lib/tcltk"}\fR.
.SS "Home directory and \fB~\fR (Tcl 9)"
.PP
Tcl 9 no longer expands a leading \fB~\fR in file commands, so the launcher expands it itself for path fields (\fBopen\fR/\fBfile\fR targets, \fBtcl\fR script/dir, each \fBtcllibpath\fR entry, and system paths like autostart). \fBhomeDir\fR returns the user's home on both Tcl 8.6 and 9 (via \fBfile home\fR, then \fBHOME\fR/\fBUSERPROFILE\fR), and \fBexpandTilde path\fR turns a leading \fB~\fR or \fB~/...\fR into an absolute path, leaving everything else (including URLs) untouched. So \fB~\fR in a config keeps working under Tcl 9.
.SS "Suggestions (not sure what to add?)"
.PP
If you are not sure which entries a menu should have, the editor can suggest some. \fBQuick add\fR inserts a sensible default set (terminal, file manager, calculator, calendar, Home). \fBSuggest...\fR opens a checklist grouped by category \-\- System tools, Power, Internet, Tcl scripts, Basics \-\- where you tick what you want and press Insert. Both add into the menu chosen in the "New in:" dropdown. The catalogue is \fBsuggestions\fR (returns \fB{category {entry ...} ...}\fR) and \fBquickAddSet\fR (the default set); both are plain data you can reuse.
